PREMISE CORRECTION FIRST. Most of this item was already fixed by two
items filed AFTER it. INV-zumin built the mechanism -- `simultaneous` as
a declared per-row marker, `simultaneous_boundaries_for` as the shared
predicate, and meta["io_boundaries"] carrying every boundary instead of
losing all but the first to YAML row order -- and INV-kaduh wired
io_mode into the C/C++ analyzers, covering this item's Shape 1. Verified
live: scala Process.apply, objc's two NSURLConnection request methods,
and erlang/elixir filelib.ensure_dir all resolve to both boundaries.

WHAT WAS ACTUALLY LEFT is one sentence of this item's own statement:
"must DECLARE what discriminates them". A census of all 15 shipped
catalogues finds 29 multi-boundary primitives -- 17 declaring a reason
(11 by mode, 6 by simultaneous) and 12 declaring NOTHING. Absence was
overloaded, meaning both:

  * RULED UNDECIDABLE -- C's unistd.write is fs_write, ipc_send or
    net_send depending on what the fd IS, established where it was
    opened. Exactly one is true; the call site cannot know which. The
    behaviour is correct and the rows say so in prose.
  * NOBODY DECIDED -- haskell's Control.Concurrent.STM.newTVar is
    declared [db_read, db_write] and creating a TVar is neither.

No consumer and no reviewer could tell those apart. Same defect shape as
the two items before it this cycle: one slot, two meanings, reader
guessing.

boundary_ruling makes the remaining two cases explicit and
multi_boundary_reason() resolves all four from one place. A
multi-boundary primitive with NO reason now fails CI rather than reading
as a deliberate undecidable.

TWO RULINGS, both from the catalogue's own evidence:

  * haskell hPutStr/hPutStrLn/hPrint/hPutChar -> call_site_undecidable.
    Their `logging` row already said why in prose -- "stderr handle used
    for logging". Which boundary is true depends on which Handle was
    passed: the same shape as unistd.write depending on the fd. That
    sentence is now machine-readable rather than a note nothing can act
    on.
  * gen_udp.open (erlang+elixir) and STM.newTVar/newTVarIO -> unruled,
    DELIBERATELY NOT RESOLVED. Opening a UDP socket ENABLES both
    directions but transfers nothing at the call, and send/recv are
    catalogued separately -- so the prior question is whether a socket
    ACQUISITION is a net_* crossing at all. For STM the prior question
    is whether db_* fits the whole IORef/STRef/MVar/STM family, which
    are in-process references, not a database. Both are recall changes
    in a precision-band cycle and both are larger than the rows they
    would touch.

`unruled` is a COUNTED value, not an escape hatch -- the
write_discipline:unaudited precedent, debt a gate reports beating debt
absence hides. The four are pinned exactly, each with its question
stated in its own row, so a new one cannot appear quietly and a resolved
one must be struck from the pin.

An unrecognised boundary_ruling value is refused at load: the row reader
takes keys with entry.get(), so an unknown key is dropped silently,
which for this field would mean a row that reads as ruled while
resolving to nothing. Rows that disagree are refused too, on the
reasoning `simultaneous` already uses.

cpp merges c.yaml and elixir merges erlang.yaml, so 6 row-groups cover
all 12 primitives. The ruling binds only multi-boundary primitives, the
same guard `simultaneous` uses, so a row grouping mixed functions is
safe.

